> Git is a free and open-source version control system that lets you track changes in your code, collaborate with others, and roll back to previous versions if needed.

>  To create a folder --->  mkdir Devops-demo

>  To go inside that folder ---> cd Devops-demo

>  To initialize empty git folder ---> git init

>  To clone existiung repository ---> git clone <copy-paste the reporsitory https:// from github>

>  To check the status ---> git status 

>  Before commiting files, we need to stage them ---> git add demo.txt

>  Committing changes ---> git commit -m "<Commit message>"

>  To remove file from local repository ---> git rm demo.txt

>  To force remove if its staged ---> git rm -f demo.txt

>  Reviewing commit history ---> git log

>  Reviewing commit history in a compact view ---> git log --oneline

>  To Review detail changes ---> git log -p

>  To visualize branches and merges  ---> git log --graph

>  To list out all the branches ---> git branch 

>  To checkout a branch or swich to another branch ---> git checkout <branch name to which you are switching to> 

>  To create and checkout/switch to another branch ---> git checkout -b <new branch name creation and switching to>

>  To merge branch into current branch ---> git merge <branch_name>

>  To delete any branch ---> git branch -d <branch_name>
   ** If you are deleting the branch in which you are currently working then it will trigger error. So, checkout from that branch to main branch and then proceed with above syntax.

>  To clear the contents on the bash ---> clear

>  To checkout to the main/master branch ---> git checkout <master/main>

>  To check if you are on the Main branch ---> git switch <master/main>

>  To fix last commit messages ---> git commit --amend -m "updated message"

>  To view the tags ---> git tag

>  To add a tag ---> git tag -a v1.0 -m "Version 1.0"







